# TASK-003: Batch Scaling Curve

Date: 2026-08-26
Hardware: NVIDIA L40S (44.4 GB VRAM)
Stack: Python 3.12.3, PyTorch 2.11.0+cu128
Method: 100 iterations per batch size, 20 warmup, synthetic random FP16 tensors

## UNet Batch Scaling

Input shape: [batch, 8, 32, 32] latent + [batch, 50, 384] audio conditioning.
Single-step (timestep=0).

| Batch | Total (ms) | Per-frame (ms) | p99/frame (ms) | Speedup vs batch=1 |
|-------|-----------|----------------|----------------|---------------------|
| 1 | 28.4 | 28.4 | 36.1 | 1.0x |
| 2 | 28.8 | 14.4 | 17.9 | 2.0x |
| 4 | 28.5 | 7.1 | 8.2 | 4.0x |
| 8 | 28.8 | 3.6 | 4.0 | 7.9x |
| 16 | 33.9 | 2.1 | 2.3 | 13.4x |

**Analysis:** UNet batch time is nearly constant from batch 1 to 8 (~28ms), meaning
the GPU is severely underutilized at small batch sizes. At batch 16 there's only 20%
overhead, achieving 13.4x per-frame throughput improvement. The L40S has ample compute
headroom for this model.

## VAE Decode Batch Scaling

Input shape: [batch, 4, 32, 32] latent.

| Batch | Total (ms) | Per-frame (ms) | Speedup vs batch=1 |
|-------|-----------|----------------|---------------------|
| 1 | 9.1 | 9.1 | 1.0x |
| 2 | 15.8 | 7.9 | 1.2x |
| 4 | 31.7 | 7.9 | 1.2x |
| 8 | 68.6 | 8.6 | 1.1x |
| 16 | 138.4 | 8.7 | 1.1x |

**Analysis:** VAE decode scales linearly with batch size — no throughput gain from
batching. This is a memory-bandwidth-bound operation. Per-frame cost is flat at ~8-9ms.
Optimization must come from compilation (torch.compile/TensorRT), not batching.

## Implications for Optimization

1. **UNet batching is free throughput** — already exploited at batch_size=8 in the
   default pipeline. Going to 16 would save another 1.5ms/frame but doubles VRAM.

2. **VAE decode is the batching-resistant bottleneck** — torch.compile or TensorRT
   are the paths forward here, not larger batch sizes.

3. **Current batch_size=8 is near-optimal** — 7.9x speedup on UNet with manageable
   VRAM. Batch 16 offers diminishing returns (13.4x vs 7.9x) at 2x memory cost.

